fact WorkItem(id: "WI-918", description: some(value: "A DOTTED PATH'S AMBIGUITY MESSAGE NAMES THE PATH BUT LISTS THE HEAD'S CANDIDATES, and the two do not visibly line up. WI-917 made a contested head reportable and reused `LoadError::AmbiguousSymbol` / the CLI's ambiguity arm unchanged, so `Widget917.w917a` is reported as `ambiguous symbol 'Widget917.w917a' … candidates [\"wi917.alpha.Widget917\", \"wi917.beta.Widget917\"]` — the candidates are the HEAD's readings (the only segment the ladder resolves), which are strictly SHORTER than the name they are candidates for. Not false, and deliberately shipped that way: the alternative readings the author could write are `wi917.alpha.Widget917.w917a` / `wi917.beta.Widget917.w917a`, and only ONE of those exists (`w917a` is alpha's), so listing both as candidates would name a path that resolves to nothing — worse than the imprecision. DRIVEN that the advice is at least true: `anthill query 'wi917.alpha.Widget917.w917a(v: ?x)'` answers, so 'Qualify the name' has somewhere to go. DECIDE whether the message should say which SEGMENT is contested (e.g. 'the head segment X of the path Y'), which needs the head string at the report site — one `split_once('.')` outside `resolve_dotted_in_kb`, i.e. a second spelling of the ladder's own dotted split, unless the ladder carries the segment in its answer. Both reporters would change together: `AmbiguousSymbol`'s two renderers in load.rs and `report_unknown_functor_name` in anthill-cli. ACCEPTANCE: either the message names the contested segment with the split spelled ONCE, or the decision to keep the current wording is recorded with its reason at the `AmbiguousSymbol` variant."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
